#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142589 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d93a0632-2268-50e0-8fd9-2f42f7822759" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "54148f16-56b3-5ead-80fd-279c9b10ad29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13699" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $4 \\times 1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7},$ expressing the result as a mixed number." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 7) + 2}{7} = \\dfrac{9}{7}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{7} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 9}{1 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{36}{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{7} = 5\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 5\\,\\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142653 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ab483d4-57af-5b1d-845f-7be648396664" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Using a Strategy"}] :question/id #uuid "cba0777d-2ad9-5135-8b1f-bde135329d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14569" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $2\\,\\dfrac 2 {9} \\times 3,$ expressing the result as a mixed number." :question/worked-solution "First, we write  $2\\,\\dfrac{2}{9}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 2 {9}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 9)+2}{9}= \\dfrac{20}{9}\n$$\n\nWe also  write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{\\color{blue}9} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{blue}9$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{red}3$) have a common factor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{9}\\times \\frac{3}{1} & =\\frac{20}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{9} \\\\  & =20\\times \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\times 1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} =6\\,\\textrm{R} 2 = 6\\,\\dfrac 2 {3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142654 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d215ffbc-b13f-5168-85d0-c75612afa8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where Simplification Is Needed"}] :question/id #uuid "2f9f1260-432a-56bc-b5e3-254b6f0fbad1" :question/math-academy-id "e-14570" :question/problem "Multiply $4 \\times 2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{4} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 9}{1 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{36}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{4} = \\dfrac{36 \\div 4}{4 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{9}{1} = 9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d93a0632-2268-50e0-8fd9-2f42f7822759" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "651fa64e-24e5-5050-97cf-defab8eed92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432986} {:answer/id #uuid "83d6cf82-ccb6-5ce3-8b1f-5da5f5e4dd28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432989} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6c6ac5-f257-5208-a9b6-bf5684ce5d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432992} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e7e052-5ff9-5234-9b35-c7ab602fdaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432999} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e3690-7acc-52fb-a6ee-a9d34c24eedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d6cf82-ccb6-5ce3-8b1f-5da5f5e4dd28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432989} :answer-field/id #uuid "c47f8ec3-ee07-5374-a258-a7ce3473e0fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b11f04e7-08c8-5f47-98ca-f9bed3019d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-152210" :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}\\times 4=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{5}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(2\\times 5)+2}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nWe also write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{1}=\\frac{12\\times 4}{5\\times 1}=\\frac{48}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $48.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ab483d4-57af-5b1d-845f-7be648396664" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Using a Strategy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87808bcc-62cf-5bcf-907c-267b5b7675b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432990} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5fde2a-b199-51ce-9701-c3abab2da3c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432991} {:answer/id #uuid "a556abeb-dc18-5303-8885-8834437da789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432996} {:answer/id #uuid "aabf682e-6c68-5e54-a165-196fe9036cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432998} {:answer/id #uuid "cc218119-3659-5c00-915c-b9f7bbfbd4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e5fde2a-b199-51ce-9701-c3abab2da3c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432991} :answer-field/id #uuid "619772b1-03bb-5668-a2bf-c48fe85c504b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c5c53ba-1c75-5a47-95b1-d73845f940f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-152213" :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n4\\times 2\\,\\frac{3}{8}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{3}{8}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{8}=\\frac{(2\\times 8)+3}{8}=\\frac{19}{8}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{19}{8}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($4$) and the second fraction's denominator ($8$) have a common \nfactor of $4.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{19}{8} & =\\frac{4}{8}\\times \\frac{19}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times 19 \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 19}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $19.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ab483d4-57af-5b1d-845f-7be648396664" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Using a Strategy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaa09e5d-517e-5def-8616-9f4be29780b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaa09e5d-517e-5def-8616-9f4be29780b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433016} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e7691d5-bf13-5c00-ad01-fd3ad7a8d134" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f78e4f9-faf2-56cf-8be7-d24c50f82032" :question/math-academy-id "q-172149" :question/problem "Expressed as a whole number, $8\\times 2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8=\\frac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{1}\\times \\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($8$) and the second fraction's denominator ($4$) have a common factor of $4.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{1}\\times \\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{8}{4}\\times \\frac{9}{1} \\\\  & =2\\times 9 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d93a0632-2268-50e0-8fd9-2f42f7822759" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6211ee24-5f4c-5aea-a7fc-6134d3d66b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6211ee24-5f4c-5aea-a7fc-6134d3d66b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432983} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10f977b-2a53-50e5-8c32-10046dd36dee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83a10579-467e-5020-9446-f0bcbebf0762" :question/math-academy-id "q-211195" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number in its lowest terms, $4\\times 1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+3}{5}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{8}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 8}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{32}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can also write this improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{5}=6\\,\\text{R}\\,2=6\\,\\frac{2}{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d93a0632-2268-50e0-8fd9-2f42f7822759" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2897c7e8-e654-5e2c-9d8f-9aae7cb64010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2897c7e8-e654-5e2c-9d8f-9aae7cb64010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432979} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4a85ed5-d400-551a-9c7d-6cfcfb070291" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2ec4fda-aa3e-5894-ae68-873ea21a07ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-211208" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number in its lowest terms, $3\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times 3=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{1}=\\frac{7\\times 3}{2\\times 1}=\\frac{21}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can also write this improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{2}=10\\,\\text{R}\\,1=10\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [