 an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{4}=\\frac{4\\times 11}{1\\times 4}=\\frac{44}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{44}{4}=\\frac{44÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{11}{1}=11\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d215ffbc-b13f-5168-85d0-c75612afa8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where Simplification Is Needed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4dc87ef-49ec-5a26-8d7d-9814e6a55889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4dc87ef-49ec-5a26-8d7d-9814e6a55889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432995} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81547da-6bae-513a-abe2-646392df8d00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d47e615f-58e6-5902-88f8-098ef1f1c1bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-211242" :question/problem "Expressed as a mixed number in its lowest terms, $3\\times 1\\,\\frac{5}{6}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{5}{6}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{(1\\times 6)+5}{6}=\\frac{11}{6}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{3\\times 11}{1\\times 6}=\\frac{33}{6}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{33}{6}=\\frac{33÷3}{6÷3}=\\frac{11}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{2}=5\\,\\text{R}\\,1=5\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d215ffbc-b13f-5168-85d0-c75612afa8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where Simplification Is Needed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95522970-c707-566a-a440-576695b2bc8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432993} {:answer/id #uuid "c128ac77-cd0b-5934-b024-b86484034e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433004} {:answer/id #uuid "dd14145d-b509-52fe-97e4-810f1cbc6439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433013} {:answer/id #uuid "df0df5b6-23d4-5fa6-8588-c23b34a81048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433014} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe57fac-7eb9-516a-8554-ba59f4a3e573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffe57fac-7eb9-516a-8554-ba59f4a3e573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433020} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c9b600f-703d-5905-bb68-4f00115ed190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b13fbae9-cf7a-599c-b37d-114ee2f7bfd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-211244" :question/problem "$2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\times 3=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(2\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1}=\\frac{8\\times 3}{3\\times 1}=\\frac{24}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{3}=\\frac{24÷3}{3÷3}=\\frac{8}{1}=8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d215ffbc-b13f-5168-85d0-c75612afa8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where Simplification Is Needed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b09085-3891-5561-b353-21ac0efa99c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b09085-3891-5561-b353-21ac0efa99c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433019} :answer-field/id #uuid "96e2c959-f14f-5ca0-ae35-a6df8db1361c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0e3e224-39c7-55c1-9017-33330a41adbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-211247" :question/problem "Expressed as a whole number, $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 4=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(1\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{1}=\\frac{5\\times 4}{4\\times 1}=\\frac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{4}=\\frac{20÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{5}{1}=5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d215ffbc-b13f-5168-85d0-c75612afa8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where Simplification Is Needed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5eda1c0-1f54-5820-bfb2-ec6dc216bc01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433000} {:answer/id #uuid "c107c182-10d5-5445-83ee-51f1cd8305b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433003} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b1c363-3d97-52db-ac92-bc64383e4842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433005} {:answer/id #uuid "d009f007-1cef-5959-8edf-02da69c9184d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433009} {:answer/id #uuid "f355bedd-82be-5022-bfa7-014ed485cc9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4b1c363-3d97-52db-ac92-bc64383e4842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433005} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce44bb81-1719-5324-9680-2e521758603f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8cf2b8e2-2314-5e9d-b171-6805247e3cfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-211257" :question/problem "$1\\,\\frac{1}{8}\\times 8=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{8}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{(1\\times 8)+1}{8}=\\frac{9}{8}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8=\\frac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{8}\\times \\frac{8}{1}=\\frac{9\\times 8}{8\\times 1}=\\frac{72}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $8:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{72}{8}=\\frac{72÷8}{8÷8}=\\frac{9}{1}=9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ab483d4-57af-5b1d-845f-7be648396664" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Using a Strategy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf7497c0-fde9-594c-af55-8f063361d71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf7497c0-fde9-594c-af55-8f063361d71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433002} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d73019f-d3f2-5f38-828d-bf81eb27a805" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bae0062b-f5ac-5baa-b921-966c2cbaf487" :question/math-academy-id "q-211376" :question/problem "Expressed as a mixed number in its lowest terms, $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\times 6=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $6$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n6=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($6$) have a common factor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1} & =\\frac{11}{1}\\times \\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =11\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\times 3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{33}{2}=16\\,\\text{R}\\,1=16\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ab483d4-57af-5b1d-845f-7be648396664" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Using a Strategy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60a6543d-449f-5db9-b18a-be345713e19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60a6543d-449f-5db9-b18a-be345713e19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432982} :answer-field/id #uuid "500fab62-64f2-56e7-a7de-5a97cade7517" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "545b365b-c759-5099-8acb-b948f190db31" :question/math-academy-id "q-211378" :question/problem "Expressed as a mixed number in its lowest terms, $1\\,\\frac{1}{20}\\times 10=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{20}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{20}=\\frac{(1\\times 20)+1}{20}=\\frac{21}{20}\n$$\n\nWe also write $10$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n10=\\frac{10}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{20}\\times \\frac{10}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($20$) and the second fraction's numerator ($10$) have a common factor of $10.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{20}\\times \\frac{10}{1} & =\\frac{21}{1}\\times \\frac{10}{20} \\\\  & =21\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\times 1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{2}=10\\,\\text{R}\\,1=10\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d93a0632-2268-50e0-8fd9-2f42f7822759" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35846e5e-d6aa-5b07-9366-d3c61775b442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432980} {:answer/id #uuid "633d3d2b-b960-5df4-8abc-904c3a72bbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432984} {:answer/id #uuid "64d583fc-0a22-540d-91f5-7806df5fab9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} 