tion as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{5}=9\\,\\text{R}3=9\\,\\frac{3}{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ab483d4-57af-5b1d-845f-7be648396664" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Using a Strategy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b86bfd33-540c-58c2-b38a-80cfd878137c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140794} {:answer/id #uuid "bc52b4cc-7496-5e3f-86e8-0f07e28bb64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140795} {:answer/id #uuid "17fb2243-ce09-559c-b76f-b9fe76d95ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140796} {:answer/id #uuid "30b78a2d-5907-51a4-a97c-e38620caec49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140797} {:answer/id #uuid "58f2025f-bb40-57f4-a0a7-b8d949abbf2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30b78a2d-5907-51a4-a97c-e38620caec49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140797} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde77874-765a-59c7-999a-80ef5d1e458c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7740ee8-6b81-5e7a-9844-9d92f4f6b3e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-57097" :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n3\\times 1\\,\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{3}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{2}{9}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{(1\\times 9)+2}{9}=\\frac{11}{9}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{9}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($3$) and the second fraction's denominator ($9$) have a common \nfactor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{9} & =\\frac{3}{9}\\times \\frac{11}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times 11 \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $11.$"}]}
